    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "211efee1_6fe8453f",
        "filename": "cmds/screencap/screencap.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 65,
      "author": {
        "id": 1012974
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-11-03T15:41:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How is it saved if neither -p or -j is specified, and the FILENAME does not end in one of these? What if they conflict? Which takes priority? (Looks like the filename.) Do we need both ways to specify the option?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 65,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 65,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "a4d44415c905be192c0e62d0e86a7163abb170c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1d8e7946_df4c53f1",
        "filename": "cmds/screencap/screencap.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 65,
      "author": {
        "id": 1244954
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-11-07T10:41:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The original approach is to output raw data if -p is not specified and name is not .png.\nAdditionally, -j and .jpeg will trigger the jpeg encoding.\n\nThere is no possibility to have 2 different extensions for a filename. It is either test.png.jpeg (which makes it a jpeg file), or test.jpeg.png (which makes it a png file).\n\nIf 100% backwards compatibility wants to be maintained, then .jpeg extensions should be encoded as raw data.\nBecause old code of \"screencap test.jpeg\" would encode as raw.\nHowever I think is really a bad idea to encode a .jpeg extensions as raw.",
      "parentUuid": "211efee1_6fe8453f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 65,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 65,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "a4d44415c905be192c0e62d0e86a7163abb170c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b6f11e53_f7b1cfcf",
        "filename": "cmds/screencap/screencap.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 65,
      "author": {
        "id": 1012974
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-11-07T14:49:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e The original approach is to output raw data if -p is not specified\n \u003e and name is not .png.\n \u003e Additionally, -j and .jpeg will trigger the jpeg encoding.\n \u003e \n \u003e There is no possibility to have 2 different extensions for a\n \u003e filename. It is either test.png.jpeg (which makes it a jpeg file),\n \u003e or test.jpeg.png (which makes it a png file).\n\nNo, but there is the possibility to use -j FILENAME.png. Looks like we override -j and save a PNG file.\n\n \u003e \n \u003e If 100% backwards compatibility wants to be maintained, then .jpeg\n \u003e extensions should be encoded as raw data.\n \u003e Because old code of \"screencap test.jpeg\" would encode as raw.\n \u003e However I think is really a bad idea to encode a .jpeg extensions\n \u003e as raw.\n\nAgreed. I don\u0027t think anyone wants that behavior (encoding raw to .jpeg extension). I just think it\u0027s a weird interface to have potentially conflicting options. Most users will likely use it sensibly. If someone does give it bad input (e.g. -j FILENAME.png or -p FILENAME.jpeg\"), perhaps we should print an error message? Or we should add a sentence to the help text specifying which one takes priority?",
      "parentUuid": "1d8e7946_df4c53f1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 65,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 65,
        "endChar": 81
      },
      "revId": "a4d44415c905be192c0e62d0e86a7163abb170c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
